<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Langres</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Andematunnum</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Langres is a hilltop town in the Haute-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marne">Marne</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> of north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, on a plateau near the sources of the Marne. As Andematunnum it was the chief town of the Gallic Lingones and then a Roman city, and from late antiquity it was the seat of a bishopric whose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> were also dukes and peers of France. Its ramparts are among the most complete in France.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/langres.jpg" alt="Langres" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Vassil · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Langres_121008_2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Langres is a commune in north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, a subprefecture of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> of Haute-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marne">Marne</a> in the region of Grand Est. The town was built on a limestone promontory of the same name. It was the capital of the Romanized Gallic tribe known as the Lingones, and was called Andematunnum, then Lingones, and now Langres. Its climate is influenced by both oceanic and humid continental patterns, with cold, snowy winters.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The stronghold was first occupied by the Lingones. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> later fortified it, at a strategic crossroads of twelve <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-roads">Roman roads</a>. The Triumphal Gate of the first century and the many objects in its museums are remains of its Gallo-Roman history. After the period of invasions the town prospered in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>, partly through the growing political influence of its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a>. The diocese covered Champagne, the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a> and Franche-Comté, and the bishops obtained the right to coin money in the ninth century and to name the city's military governor in 927. The Bishop of Langres was a duke and peer of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>.</p><p>The troubled 14th and 15th centuries led the town to strengthen its defences, and it entered a period of royal tutelage. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> brought a return of prosperity and the construction of many civil, religious and military buildings that still stand. A &quot;Vauban&quot; citadel was added in the 19th century.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The historic town centre is surrounded by defensive walls with a dozen towers and seven gates. The cathedral of Saint-Mammès, dedicated to Mammes of Caesarea, a martyr of the 3rd century, dates from the late 12th century. The town gives its name to an AOC-protected soft cow's milk cheese with a washed rind. It was long known for its cutlery industry, and Didier Diderot, father of the encyclopedist Denis, was a cutler. Denis Diderot was born in Langres in 1713, and a museum, the Denis Diderot House of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/age-of-enlightenment">Enlightenment</a>, opened there in 2013 in a private mansion of the 16th and 18th centuries.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Langres” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Robert’s descendants ruled from Dijon until 1361, and the duchy kept its lasting core between the Saône, the Morvan and the plateau of <b>Langres</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-burgundy">Duchy of Burgundy · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Diderot, the son of a cutler from <b>Langres</b>, was imprisoned at Vincennes in 1749 for his writings and gave a quarter of a century to the Encyclopédie.</blockquote><a href="/movement/enlightenment">Enlightenment · People</a></li><li><blockquote>Their chalk plains were rich farmland on the route between Reims and <b>Langres</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/catalauni">Catalauni</a></li><li><blockquote>The Lingones held the uplands around <b>Andematunnum</b> (Langres), between the Sequani, the Aedui and the Leuci, astride the watershed between the Seine and Saône basins.</blockquote><a href="/state/lingones">Lingones</a></li><li><blockquote>Ellwangen abbey was founded around 764 by Hariolf, later bishop of <b>Langres</b>, and received Carolingian protection.</blockquote><a href="/state/prince-provostry-of-ellwangen">Prince-Provostry of Ellwangen</a></li></ul></section>
